{"author":{"id":"127a58c7f014820991be95805df42525db12c92e2d7319d04d4df105a9aa3444"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1788227300,"nonce":"Rcu5VOihZ6sjlDjJtSYtLPlN1bY=","message":"RESOLVED — Evan authorised the force-push, 2026-08-31. Store is clean locally AND on the remote, verified from a fresh clone.\n\n\n1. **The five unreadable merges were parked on the remote FIRST**, at `refs/backup/s11-unreadable-gitbug-merge/\u003cid\u003e`. This is what made the force-push safe rather than destructive: the merge commits stay permanently referenced, so nothing became eligible for garbage collection.\n\n   | bug | preserved merge |\n   |---|---|\n   | `20e5cda` | `df6b25d21880676e35401d5dd3b4356606f5fcef` |\n   | `70ac258` | `26ef66be079cf37024b74ba9ad93be1a5fea2316` |\n   | `96d9315` | `6d5af6efb841f6c6835f202030cb1aafdc5a2502` |\n   | `d2e1229` | `11f8bec7e7a91ba36d7b6060665148bb9fb37395` |\n   | `fbb2c59` | `b6cf1df2dd5222b72deee67259ba64cb657f86ed` |\n\n2. The five `refs/bugs/*` were then force-pushed to their readable state. Remote now matches local on all five.\n\n3. **Verified on a FRESH CLONE**, which is what BORGES actually gets — not on this working copy. `git-bug bug` reads clean, 58 bugs, exit 0.\n\n\nThe body says \"no data is lost — every operation is present in one parent or the other.\" True of the OBJECTS, and misleading about the REFS. Resetting each ref to one parent made the other parent's operations invisible to the store, and they were not all duplicates.\n\nChecked rather than assumed, by diffing comment ids between each dropped parent and each kept head. **Six comments existed only on the dropped side:**\n\n| bug | comment | what it was |\n|---|---|---|\n| `20e5cda` | `2000e05` | the fix-round writeup on branch `fix/3.5`, including a DESIGN.md correction |\n| `96d9315` | `916fd99` | the closing rationale — acceptance is a dry-run, no bench owed |\n| `d2e1229` | `d924ed1` | Evan's ruling making draft→fight→undraft a hard criterion on 4.3 |\n| `fbb2c59` | `f9b9b42` | the handover to session 10's ledger |\n| `fbb2c59` | `feb1b02` | 2.5's mechanical acceptance run, first execution of MapDumpVerbs/CatalogDump |\n| `70ac258` | `7e0badc` | session 11's own close — already re-applied, a genuine duplicate |\n\n**Five were real losses and have been restored**, reproduced verbatim with a header naming their original id and the backup ref they came from. The sixth was a duplicate and was deliberately not re-posted. All five confirmed present on the remote copy from the fresh clone.\n\nThat the claim was wrong is the more useful record here: \"the objects still exist\" and \"the store still shows them\" are different statements, and only the second is what anyone reading the tracker experiences.\n\n\n- \"A check exists that a bug store is readable AFTER a pull and BEFORE a push.\" **`git-bug bug \u003e/dev/null 2\u003e\u00261` is the whole test**, and it is now recorded here and in RUNLOG session 11. Not automated — deliberately, because there is no hook point this project owns that runs around `git-bug pull`. Whoever pulls runs it.\n- **One methodological trap worth keeping:** the first bisect blamed `20e5cda` alone and then \"proved\" all 17 of its commits unreadable. That was a confound — a single bad ref poisons every subsequent test, so nothing can be bisected until the set is otherwise clean. The correct procedure is to empty the ref set and add one ref at a time, dropping each that breaks the store.\n- Upstream cause unfixed and not ours: `git-bug` `dev-f2070b5325`, `entity/dag.read` / `ReadAll`. Worth checking a newer release before this recurs; it will recur on the next diverged pair.","files":null}]}